# Consensus plan — independent review and author revision
jstack adaptation of [OMC Ralplan at 5281b19](https://github.com/Yeachan-Heo/oh-my-claudecode/blob/5281b19e0d64f8e6dc6767f2130299a88af2dc71/skills/ralplan/SKILL.md),
with [the original MIT notice](consensus-LICENSE.txt). Read this entire recipe
when `consensus-plan` or jstack `plan --consensus` is explicitly selected, by the
user's request or by a workflow the user started. Ordinary planning does not
require a panel. Exact OMC `ralplan` or `oh-my-claudecode:plan` remains an
upstream provider request, including its mandatory terminal/handoff behavior.
If that provider or a required independent assessor is unavailable, return that
status; never relabel one author's roleplay as independent consensus.
A single independent attempt to refute a proposal or result, with no revision
loop, is a `second-opinion` request. This recipe uses the same reviewer
separation (each assessor sees the same artifact and no other assessor's
output) and adds fixed alternatives, author-only synthesis and a finite
re-review loop. Like a `second-opinion` verdict, its consensus is evidence, not
permission to execute.
## Freeze the question, alternatives and budget
The planner identifies scope, caller, repository/source revision, constraints
and requested planning artifact. Standalone and external calls create no global
jstack task and do not take over the caller's coordinator. This recipe may write
requested plan/proposal artifacts but cannot authorize implementation, commits,
publication or execution delegation.
Before reviews, record three to five design principles, the three most important
decision drivers and at least two substantive viable alternatives with bounded
advantages and disadvantages. If constraints eliminate an alternative, show its
actual invalidation evidence; do not disguise superficial variations as options.
A plan with no fair comparison is not review-ready. For high-risk or explicitly
deliberate work, include three concrete failure scenarios and the required unit,
integration, end-to-end and observability validation (or justified inapplicability).
At selection fix finite limits: at most five total review rounds and at most two
invalid rounds by default. The caller may select stricter or different finite
limits before execution; record the departure from upstream's five iterations.
One total round includes the initial pair of assessments or one pair of complete
re-assessments; the initial review consumes round one. Upstream says five loop
iterations without making initial-review inclusion explicit. jstack deliberately
uses this stricter total-round definition rather than asserting equal budgets.
Also record any elapsed-time/token budget available and an explicit convergence
criterion: both required independent assessments complete on the same unchanged
revision, the criteria assessor approves, and the author has addressed every
blocking antithesis with evidence. The invalid-round count is part of the total,
not additional free retries. No restart, correction or resume resets these counters.
Write or retain one immutable plan snapshot with a revision and SHA-256 digest.
The coordinator computes the digest over the exact UTF-8 file bytes delivered
to both assessors, including any wrapper text and line endings. Record that byte
boundary in a separate receipt; do not embed a self-referential authoritative
digest in the plan. An author's inline digest is not the validation authority.
If it conflicts with the receipt, preserve the discrepancy and resolve which
bytes will be reviewed before dispatch. The criteria/context receipt likewise
identifies the exact supplied inputs rather than a prose claim of equivalence.
Freeze the criteria and context supplied to assessors with it. Record actual
host/provider/session or execution identities for planner and each assessor,
requested and observed model/effort when available, exact input digest, allowed
tools and output artifacts. Distinct role names, prose claims or equal prompts
alone do not prove independent actors or private context. If host evidence cannot
establish separate executions and absence of peer context, return unverified.
Do not infer independence from matching digests while peer output is exposed in
shared files, inherited chat or imported instructions.
## Review one unchanged revision
Before the first dispatch, verify that two separate read-only assessor executions
are actually available and can receive isolated inputs. If no such execution
path exists, return unavailable with the best current proposal; consume no round
and do not narrate or simulate completed reviews. If executions exist but their
identity or isolation cannot be evidenced, return unverified before dispatch.
Only actual dispatch begins a round; once begun, interrupted or invalid work
remains charged to the original finite budget.
1. Dispatch the structural assessor with the frozen plan and criteria, without
any other assessor's response. It returns the strongest steelman objection,
at least one genuine trade-off, potential synthesis, blockers and evidence.
The assessor is read-only and cannot change the plan.
2. Await that result, then dispatch a separate criteria assessor with the same
original plan/context snapshot, never the first response. It evaluates
principle-option consistency, fair alternatives, mitigations, testable
acceptance and concrete verification; deliberate mode also checks the
failure scenarios and expanded validation. It returns APPROVE, ITERATE or
REJECT with reasons. Await it individually. This jstack pilot preserves the
upstream sequential ordering; a parallel variant is not claimed equivalent.
3. The planner alone collects and synthesizes both completed results. Preserve
disagreements, counterevidence and provenance rather than averaging verdicts
or treating a majority as truth. Missing reviews are unavailable/partial,
not approval. A council bridge returning one successful response does not
satisfy this two-assessor requirement.
Check the plan/context digest before and after each review and check actual
execution identities and peer-isolation evidence. A plan changed mid-round,
different digest, leaked peer output, reused author actor or unverified context
invalidates the whole round. Preserve the invalid outputs as evidence, discard
their verdicts, charge both the total and invalid budgets and retry only within
the original remaining limits. Reaching the invalid limit returns partial or
unavailable with its cause; it cannot certify consensus.
## Author revision and full re-review
For ITERATE, REJECT or an unresolved substantive objection, the planner writes
an explicit disposition per finding, revises the complete plan and creates a
new immutable revision/digest. Both assessors review that entire revised plan,
not only changed fragments. Do not mix an older approval with a newer plan.
The author cannot edit assessor verdicts, suppress dissent or manufacture an
approval. Keep the best coherent proposal and unresolved objections available
throughout the loop, so a stop never loses the useful result.
## Termination, cancellation and continuation
At convergence return the reviewed proposal, selected scope/revision/digest,
alternatives and criteria, decision/drivers/consequences/follow-ups, actual
assessment receipts, finding dispositions, remaining dissent and the next
consumer. Review consensus is not human acceptance or permission to execute.
Default output remains pending approval where human acceptance is required;
reuse valid existing scope/revision-bound authorization without redundant asking.
A separate selected executor receives any already-authorized next work; this
planning recipe stops and does not implement it itself.
At budget exhaustion, max rounds, unavailable assessor or user stop, return the
best plan plus unresolved disagreement and exact pending/unavailable/cancelled
status. Do not force consensus or continue endlessly. Cancellation takes
precedence over completing a round. Preserve pending processes and uncertain
external effects; report cleanup failures rather than pretending they ended.
For compaction, resume or caller handoff retain the frozen recipe/budgets and
consumed counters, plan and source digests, actor/input receipts, acceptance
source, completed/invalid rounds, unreviewed findings and processes/effects.
Recheck source and input identity plus isolation capability before resuming.
Missing records leave consensus unverified; a stale completion signal or prior
assistant summary cannot substitute for the actual round evidence. A changed
scope needs explicit new scope accounting, not a covert budget reset.
## Differences from upstream
The alternatives, steelman, independent sequential criteria review, planner-only
synthesis and whole-plan re-review are preserved. jstack adds explicit isolation
verification, invalid-round budgets and bounded interruption/external return.
The total-round limit counts the initial assessment pair; it is not a claim that
upstream's ambiguously described iteration count has identical semantics.
OMC's global keyword gate, company-context lookup and automatic Team/Ralph
handoff are not imported. Exact-provider nested execution additionally needs
artifact and mandatory-next/terminal closure; an artifact alone does not prove it.
